module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.FamilyFibreDivisors
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.IntegralLocallyPrincipal

/-!
# The two distinguished divisors of an integral family are effective Cartier

Let `f : X ⟶ S` and let `W ⊆ X ×_S ℙ¹_S` be an integral closed subscheme which is contained in
neither the image of the zero section nor the image of the infinity section. Part (1) of Stacks
Tag 02S4 says that `W₀ = W ∩ D₀` and `W_∞ = W ∩ D_∞` are then effective Cartier divisors on `W`.

This file assembles that conclusion from the nondegeneracy hypothesis and *one* remaining input:
that the divisor is locally principal. Nondegeneracy gives `W₀ ≠ W`
(`Stacks.Chow.zeroDivisor_ne_bot`), and on an integral scheme a locally principal closed
subscheme other than the whole scheme is an effective Cartier divisor
(`Stacks.Divisors.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_of_isLocallyPrincipal`), because the local generator
is then automatically a nonzerodivisor.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Chow.zeroDivisor_isEffectiveCartierDivisor_of_isLocallyPrincipal` and
  `Stacks.Chow.infinityDivisor_isEffectiveCartierDivisor_of_isLocallyPrincipal`: part (1) of
  Stacks Tag 02S4, granted local principality of the divisor.

## Implementation notes

**What remains for Tag 02S4 (1), exactly.** The hypothesis `IsLocallyPrincipal (zeroDivisor f W)`
is the affine-local description of the family: on an affine open of `W` lying inside the first
chart of `ℙ¹`, the ideal of `W₀` is generated by the image of the coordinate of that chart. The
statement is recorded here as an explicit argument rather than proved, in the style of the rest of
the chapter-42 development, so that the tag is reduced to a single named input instead of a
three-step informal plan.

**Why that input cannot be obtained by pulling back along `W.subschemeι`.** The ideal sheaf being
pulled back is `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.vanishingIdeal`, the *reduced* structure
on the closure of the range of the section, and the reduced structure on the image of the zero
section is **not** locally principal on `X ×_S ℙ¹_S` unless `X` is reduced: on a chart with ring
`A[T]` the vanishing ideal of `V(T)` is `{g : g(0) nilpotent}`, and for `A = k[ε]/(ε²)` that is
`(T, ε)`, which is not principal in `A[T]`. So
`Stacks.Divisors.isLocallyPrincipal_comap` applied to the ambient vanishing ideal is not a route
to the hypothesis, and the argument must use the reducedness of `W` itself — which is what the
implementation notes of `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Chow/FamilyFibreDivisors.lean` describe:
the elements of the vanishing ideal that are not multiples of the coordinate have nilpotent
constant term and so die in `W`.

Reducedness of `W` is not assumed separately: `AlgebraicGeometry.isReduced_of_isIntegral` supplies
it from integrality, which is the hypothesis the tag states.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 02S4](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02S4)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01WR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01WR)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory

universe u

namespace Stacks.Chow

variable {X S : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ S)
  (W : (projectiveLineProduct f).IdealSheafData)

/-- **Stacks Project, Tag 02S4, part (1), for the zero divisor, granted local principality.**
If `W` is integral and is not contained in the image of the zero section, and if the zero divisor
of `W` is locally principal, then it is an effective Cartier divisor on `W`: the nondegeneracy
hypothesis makes it a *proper* closed subscheme, and on an integral scheme that forces the local
generator to be a nonzerodivisor. -/
theorem zeroDivisor_isEffectiveCartierDivisor_of_isLocallyPrincipal [IsIntegral W.subscheme]
    (h : ¬ Set.range W.subschemeι ⊆ Set.range (zeroSection f))
    (hprin : Stacks.Divisors.IsLocallyPrincipal (zeroDivisor f W)) :
    Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor (zeroDivisor f W) :=
  Stacks.Divisors.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_of_isLocallyPrincipal _
    (zeroDivisor_ne_bot f W h) hprin

/-- **Stacks Project, Tag 02S4, part (1), for the infinity divisor, granted local
principality.** The same argument as for the zero divisor, with the infinity section in place of
the zero section. -/
theorem infinityDivisor_isEffectiveCartierDivisor_of_isLocallyPrincipal [IsIntegral W.subscheme]
    (h : ¬ Set.range W.subschemeι ⊆ Set.range (infinitySection f))
    (hprin : Stacks.Divisors.IsLocallyPrincipal (infinityDivisor f W)) :
    Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor (infinityDivisor f W) :=
  Stacks.Divisors.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_of_isLocallyPrincipal _
    (infinityDivisor_ne_bot f W h) hprin

end Stacks.Chow

end
